From 39d0da8737b39c6cf5ce4328398412d14ad09e12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: blueworkslabs <blueworkslabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] MediaCodec: put MediaTek HEVC pre-keyframe pictures back in
 order

Unofficial workaround, not part of Kodi.

OMX.MTK.VIDEO.DECODER.HEVC (seen on a Fire TV Stick 4K Max, 2nd gen)
now and then emits the last pictures before a keyframe out of order: the
picture with the highest pts first, with its own pts, then the earlier
ones with invented, increasing pts (previous + ~16 ms). On screen the
picture jumps ahead and back (A D B C E instead of A B C D E).

CMediaCodecOutputReorder keeps a ledger of the pts queued to the decoder,
holds the early picture, gives the late pictures with an invented label
(12-20 ms steps, below the next queued picture) the missing pts in
ascending order and hands the held picture over after them. Anything
else is handed over in the decoder's order; a picture without pts, a
drain or a pts-less input stops reordering until the next flush. A held
picture is handed over after at most 250 ms or at end of stream, and
dropped on flush.

Enabled only for surface rendering, mime video/hevc and that decoder;
Dolby Vision content is left alone.
